@Tutorial(time: 25) {
    @Intro(title: "Designing the mob scene") {
        In this part, you're going to code the monsters, which we'll call mobs. In
        the next lesson, we'll spawn them randomly around the playable area.

        We'll build the mob scene the same way we built the player: a wrapper
        scene that inherits the `.glb`, and a gameplay scene that instances it
        under a `Pivot`. We'll also add a fake blob shadow under the mob so
        it's easier to read its position on the ground.

        We won't add the gameplay collision shapes for hitting or squashing
        the mob in this lesson — that comes in a later lesson dedicated to
        hit boxes and hurt boxes. For now, the mob just needs to look right
        and move.
    }
    @Section(title: "Creating a wrapper scene for the mob model") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Just like the player, the mob model lives in a `.glb` file:
            `art/enemy_snake.glb`. We'll create a wrapper scene that inherits
            it, so we can scale and orient the model without touching the
            original file.

            The snake's `Move` animation should also loop seamlessly. We'll
            configure that in the import settings.

            @Image(source: 04-01.mob-wrapper-scene-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In the _File Pad_, locate `art/enemy_snake.glb`.
                
                Double tap it to open the _Advanced Import Settings_ window.
                @Image(source: 04-01a.advanced-import-window-main.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Got to the **Move** animation node and open the _Inspector_.
                
                Set the **Loop Mode** to `Linear`; then press **Reimport**.
                @Image(source: 04-01b.advanced-import-window-main.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Tap `art/enemy_snake.glb` once, then press **Open Scene** in the _Inspector_. 
                
                In the pop-up, press **New Inherited**.

                @Image(source: 04-02.new-inherited-snake-scene.png, alt: "Long-press enemy_snake.glb and choose New Inherited Scene")
            }
            @Step {
                Save the new scene as `enemy_snake.tscn`, inside the `scenes/`
                folder we made earlier.

                @Image(source: 04-03.save-enemy-snake-scene.png, alt: "Save the inherited scene as scenes/enemy_snake.tscn")
            }
            @Step {
                In the _Node Tree_, select the `Armature` node. In the
                _Inspector_ using the **Transform** parameter, scale it by `2.5` on all axes and rotate it
                `180` degrees around Y, just like we did for the player. This
                makes the snake face -Z and gives it a sensible size.

                @Image(source: 04-04.scale-rotate-snake-armature.png, alt: "Scale the Armature 2.5 and rotate 180 on Y")
            }
            @Step {
                Select the `AnimationPlayer` node.
                Open the Animation window at the bottom of the screen and tap the `A` next to `Move` to turn on `Autoplay` so the snake
                starts crawling as soon as it spawns.

                @Image(source: 04-05.autoplay-move-animation.png, alt: "Set the AnimationPlayer autoplay to Move")
            }
            @Step {
                Save the `enemy_snake.tscn` wrapper scene.
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Creating the mob gameplay scene") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Now we'll create the `Mob` scene that we'll instantiate from code
            when spawning enemies.

            @Image(source: 04-07.mob-gameplay-scene-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create a `Empty` scene named `mob` in the `scenes` folder we made earlier and then add a
                [CharacterBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_characterbody3d.html#class-characterbody3d)
                node as its root. Name the node `Mob` as well.

                @Image(source: 04-08.create-mob-character-body.png, alt: "A new scene with a CharacterBody3D root node named Mob")
            }
            @Step {
                Select the root and add a child node
                [Node3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_node3d.html#class-node3d), naming it `Pivot`.

                @Image(source: 04-09.add-pivot-to-mob.png, alt: "A Node3D child named Pivot added to Mob")
            }
            @Step {
                With the `Pivot` node selected, instantiate a child scene and
                pick `art/enemy_snake.tscn`. Rename the instantiated node to
                `Character`.

                @Image(source: 04-10.initial-mob-scene-tree.png)
            }
            We'll add a fake blob shadow next, then a node to detect when the
            mob leaves the screen.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Adding a blob shadow") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Because we use an orthographic camera looking down at an angle,
            real-time shadows can be hard to read. A simple trick is to add a
            flat, semi-transparent dark circle under the mob — a "blob
            shadow." It grounds the character visually and helps the player
            judge where the mob is.

            The starter project includes a gradient texture for exactly this
            purpose, in `resources/CircleGrandient.tres`.

            @Image(source: 04-11.blob-shadow-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Select the `Mob` node and add a child
                [Sprite3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_sprite3d.html#class-sprite3d).
                This node draws a 2D texture in 3D space.

                @Image(source: 04-12.add-sprite3d-node.png, alt: "Add a Sprite3D child to the Mob node")
            }
            @Step {
                In the _Inspector_, assign `resources/CircleGrandient.tres` to
                the _Texture_ property.

                @Image(source: 04-13.assign-circle-gradient.png, alt: "Assign the CircleGrandient texture to the Sprite3D")
            }
            @Step {
                Set the _Modulate_ color to black and drag the _Transparency_
                slider so the shadow is about 30% opaque. Set the _Scale_ to
                about `8` on X and Y so the blob is wider than the snake, and
                rotate the sprite `90` degrees on X so it lies flat on the
                ground.

                > Tip: Disable the sprite's _Cast Shadow_ property so the blob
                > doesn't cast its own real-time shadow on top of itself.

                @Image(source: 04-14.sprite3d-shadow-settings.png, alt: "Tint the sprite black, make it transparent, scale and rotate flat")
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Removing monsters off-screen") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We're going to spawn monsters at regular time intervals in the game
            level. If we're not careful, their count could increase to infinity,
            and we don't want that. Each mob instance has both a memory and a
            processing cost, and we don't want to pay for it when the mob is
            outside the screen.

            Once a monster leaves the screen, we don't need it anymore, so we
            should delete it. Xogot has a node that detects when objects leave
            the screen,
            [VisibleOnScreenNotifier3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_visibleonscreennotifier3d.html#class-visibleonscreennotifier3d),
            and we're going to use it to destroy our mobs.

            > Note: When you keep instancing an object, there's a technique you
            > can use to avoid the cost of creating and destroying instances
            > all the time called pooling. It consists of pre-creating an
            > array of objects and reusing them over and over.
            >
            > When working with GDScript, you don't need to worry about this.
            > The main reason to use pools is to avoid freezes with
            > garbage-collected languages. GDScript uses a different technique
            > to manage memory, reference counting, which doesn't have that
            > caveat. You can learn more about that here:
            > [Memory management](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/gdscript/gdscript_basics.html#doc-gdscript-basics-memory-management).

            @Image(source: 04-15.visibility-notifier-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Select the `Mob` node and add a child node
                [VisibleOnScreenNotifier3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_visibleonscreennotifier3d.html#class-visibleonscreennotifier3d).
                Another box, pink this time, appears. When this box completely
                leaves the screen, the node will emit a signal.

                @Image(source: 04-16.visibility-notifier-added.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Resize it using the orange dots until it covers the entire 3D
                model.

                @Image(source: 04-17.visibility-bbox-resized.png)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Coding the mob's movement") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Let's implement the monster's motion. We're going to do this in two
            steps. First, we'll write a script on the `Mob` that defines a
            function to initialize the monster. We'll then code the randomized
            spawn mechanism in the `main.tscn` scene and call the function from
            there.

            @Image(source: 04-18.mob-movement-code-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Attach a script to the `Mob`.

                @Image(source: 04-19.mob-attach-script.png)
            }
            @Step {
                We define two properties, `min_speed` and `max_speed`, to define
                a random speed range, which we will later use to define
                `CharacterBody3D.velocity`.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-3d-00.gd, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                Add a `_physics_process` function that calls `move_and_slide()`.
                Similarly to the player, we move the mob every frame by calling
                the function `CharacterBody3D.move_and_slide()`. This time, we
                don't update the `velocity` every frame; we want the monster
                to move at a constant speed and leave the screen, even if it
                were to hit an obstacle.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-3d-01.gd, previousFile: mob-3d-00.gd)
            }
            We need to define another function to calculate the
            `CharacterBody3D.velocity`. This function will turn the monster
            towards the player and randomize both its angle of motion and its
            velocity. The function will take a `start_position`, the mob's
            spawn position, and the `player_position` as its arguments.

            @Step {
                We position the mob at `start_position` and turn it towards
                the player using the `look_at_from_position()` method, and
                randomize the angle by rotating a random amount around the Y
                axis. Below, `randf_range()` outputs a random value between
                `-PI / 4` radians and `PI / 4` radians.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-3d-02.gd, previousFile: mob-3d-01.gd)
            }
            We got a random position, now we need a `random_speed`.
            `randi_range()` will be useful as it gives random int values, and
            we will use `min_speed` and `max_speed`. `random_speed` is just an
            integer, and we just use it to multiply our
            `CharacterBody3D.velocity`. After `random_speed` is applied, we
            rotate `CharacterBody3D.velocity` Vector3 towards the player.

            @Step {
                Add the random speed calculation and rotate the velocity
                vector based on the mob's Y rotation in order to move in the
                direction the mob is looking.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-3d-03.gd, previousFile: mob-3d-02.gd)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Leaving the screen") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We still have to destroy the mobs when they leave the screen. To do
            so, we'll connect our
            [VisibleOnScreenNotifier3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_visibleonscreennotifier3d.html#class-visibleonscreennotifier3d)
            node's `screen_exited` signal to the `Mob`.

            @Image(source: 04-20.screen-exit-signal-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Head back to the 3D viewport by tapping on the _3D_ label at
                the top of the editor.

                @Image(source: 04-21.switch-to-3d-workspace.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Select the
                [VisibleOnScreenNotifier3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_visibleonscreennotifier3d.html#class-visibleonscreennotifier3d)
                node and, in the _Signals_ tab, double-tap the `screen_exited()`
                signal.

                @Image(source: 04-22.signals-tab-screen-exited.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Connect the signal to the `Mob`.

                @Image(source: 04-23.connect-signal-to-mob.png)
            }
            This will add a new function for you in your mob script,
            `_on_visible_on_screen_notifier_3d_screen_exited()`. From it, call
            the `queue_free()` method. This function destroys the instance
            it's called on.

            @Step {
                The connected handler calls `queue_free()` to free the node
                when it leaves the screen.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-3d-04.gd, previousFile: mob-3d-03.gd)
            }
            Our monster is ready to enter the game! In the next part, you
            will spawn monsters in the game level.
        }
    }
}
